{"id":"h059","run":2,"role":"calibration","category":"science-physics","question":"An electron is released from rest in a uniform electric field of magnitude $2.00 \\times 10^4 \\mathrm{~N} / \\mathrm{C}$. Calculate the acceleration of the electron. (Ignore gravitation.)\nIs the correct answer \"$3.51$$10^{15} \\mathrm{~m} / \\mathrm{s}^2$\"?","truth":"yes","model_answer":"yes","correct":true,"source":"TIGER-Lab/MMLU-Pro, test split","claims":[{"text":"The electron's acceleration is given by a = qE/m.","confidence":0.98,"lean":1,"theta":1.5394,"phi":0.2206},{"text":"The force on a charge in a uniform field is F = qE, and Newton's second law gives the acceleration.","confidence":0.97,"lean":1,"theta":1.5237,"phi":0.203},{"text":"Gravity can be ignored, as the problem specifies.","confidence":0.95,"lean":1,"theta":1.4923,"phi":0.0},{"text":"With e = 1.60e-19 C and m = 9.11e-31 kg, the acceleration is about 3.51e15 m/s².","confidence":0.95,"lean":1,"theta":1.4923,"phi":0.1717},{"text":"The computed result matches the given value of 3.51e15 m/s².","confidence":0.95,"lean":1,"theta":1.4923,"phi":-0.0017},{"text":"Using more precise CODATA constants gives about 3.52e15 m/s², differing slightly from the given answer.","confidence":0.85,"lean":-1,"theta":1.3352,"phi":1.9243},{"text":"The difference between 3.51 and 3.52 is only a last-digit rounding artifact of the constants, not a real discrepancy.","confidence":0.9,"lean":1,"theta":1.4137,"phi":0.3208},{"text":"The physics is unambiguous.","confidence":0.9,"lean":1,"theta":1.4137,"phi":0.0062},{"text":"The given answer is the standard result using the common three-significant-figure constants, so it is correct.","confidence":0.92,"lean":1,"theta":1.4451,"phi":0.027}],"relations":[{"i":0,"j":1,"J":0.8,"relation":"supports"},{"i":0,"j":3,"J":0.7,"relation":"supports"},{"i":0,"j":4,"J":0.6,"relation":"supports"},{"i":0,"j":5,"J":0.5,"relation":"supports"},{"i":0,"j":8,"J":0.5,"relation":"supports"},{"i":1,"j":3,"J":0.6,"rela